  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe strategy leverages quaternary sequences, known for their controlled autocorrelation properties, to construct h and minimize overlap. By using a combinatorial design, we avoid the restrictive symmetry of previous methods and potentially escape their local optima.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Define h: [0, 2] \u2192 [0,1], sampled at n_points locations.\n- Use a predefined quaternary sequence q \u2208 {0,1,2,3}^n_points.\n- Scale q: h_i = (q_i - 1)/2 to map into [0,1].\n- Adjust h to meet sum(h) = n_points/2; maintain 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1.\n- Compute cross-correlation between h and (1 - h), scale by dx, and find max.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Read initial_h_values and set n_points = len(initial_h_values).\nb. Generate a base quaternary sequence promoting low overlap.\nc. Scale to [0,1], adjust sum, ensuring constraints.\nd. Compute cross-correlation to evaluate C\u2085.\ne. Refine with perturbations and optimizations.\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Use predefined sequences and systematic perturbations for exploration.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEstimated runtime within 1100s with early stopping. Checks ensure feasibility and handle failures.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nConfirm constraints met, compute C\u2085, and return valid solution.",
